    <!-- 22 ATOMIC MODEL development (teach: model) -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="model">
      <span class="tag lo">Developing the atomic model</span>
      <h2>How the model was built</h2>
      <p>The model of the atom changed as new <b>evidence</b> arrived — a great example of how science self-corrects:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Plum pudding (Thomson)</b> — the atom is a ball of positive charge with electrons dotted through it.</li>
        <li><b>Rutherford (alpha-scattering, with Geiger &amp; Marsden)</b> — firing alpha particles at gold foil, most passed straight through, but a few bounced back. This showed the atom is <b>mostly empty space</b> with a tiny, dense, positively charged <b>nucleus</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Bohr</b> — refined this: electrons orbit in fixed <b>energy levels (shells)</b> at set distances, which explained why atoms were stable.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="The alpha-scattering experiment: most alpha particles pass through gold foil, a few deflect">
          <rect x="20" y="88" width="46" height="34" rx="6" fill="#2c2840"/>
          <text x="43" y="138" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="10" fill="#2c2840">α source</text>
          <rect x="300" y="35" width="10" height="140" fill="#ffd166" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="305" y="190" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="10" fill="#9a5a00">gold foil</text>
          <circle cx="305" cy="105" r="7" fill="#c8203a" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="305" y="28" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="9" fill="#c8203a">nucleus</text>
          <!-- straight through -->
          <line x1="66" y1="95" x2="540" y2="80" stroke="#34c777" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <polygon points="540,80 524,78 526,89" fill="#34c777"/>
          <line x1="66" y1="115" x2="540" y2="128" stroke="#34c777" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <polygon points="540,128 526,118 524,129" fill="#34c777"/>
          <!-- small deflection -->
          <line x1="66" y1="105" x2="305" y2="105" stroke="#2e8bff" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <line x1="305" y1="105" x2="500" y2="55" stroke="#2e8bff" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <polygon points="500,55 486,60 491,68" fill="#2e8bff"/>
          <!-- bounce back -->
          <line x1="66" y1="103" x2="298" y2="104" stroke="#ff5b6e" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <line x1="298" y1="104" x2="80" y2="160" stroke="#ff5b6e" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <polygon points="80,160 96,156 92,167" fill="#ff5b6e"/>
          <text x="420" y="150" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">most pass straight through</text>
          <text x="150" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" fill="#c8203a">a few bounce back</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Alpha-scattering: most particles miss everything (empty space); the rare back-scatters reveal a tiny, dense, positive nucleus.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="note"><b>Energy levels:</b> an electron can move <b>up</b> a level by <b>absorbing</b> electromagnetic radiation, and falls back <b>down</b> by <b>emitting</b> EM radiation. The energy of the radiation matches the gap between the levels.</p>
    </section>

